duplicates.pl---->
The dupli predicate checks whether a given list contains duplicate elements. 
It recursively traverses the list and uses the built-in member predicate to detect duplicates.

How It Works

The predicate checks if the head of the list (H) is a member of the tail (T). If a match is found, it immediately returns true (due to the cut operator !).
If the head is unique, the predicate recursively checks the remaining elements.
An empty list ([]) does not have duplicates, so it returns false.


Running in SWI-Prolog

Save the code in a file named duplicates.pl.
Open SWI-Prolog and load the file:
        ?- [duplicates].
Run queries:    
        ?- dupli([1, 2, 3, 4, 1]).   




Notes:
 % is a comment  symbol in prolog , so to use this  use '%' instead .
 Also , it treats 11 and 11.0 differently , 














sqrt.pl--->

 This Prolog program calculates the square root of a given number using the Newton-Raphson method (also called the Babylonian method). 
 The algorithm iteratively refines an initial guess until the approximation is within a given accuracy 

 How It Works

The squareroot predicate:
Takes a number X, an accuracy Accuracy, and returns the Result.
Uses an initial guess (X / 3) to start the approximation.
Calls iterate_sqrt/4 to refine the approximation iteratively.
The iterate_sqrt predicate:
If the difference between Guess^2 and X is smaller than Accuracy, it stops and returns Guess.
Otherwise, it refines the guess using the formula:
Calls itself recursively until the stopping condition is met.




Running in SWI-Prolog

Save the code in a file named sqrt.pl.
Open SWI-Prolog and load the file:
?- [sqrt].



Run queries:  first parameter is whose squareroot we are finding , second on is result storing varable name , and third is accuracy/precision with result we want 


?- squareroot(25, Result, 0.0001).
Result = 5.000000000053722.

?- squareroot(49, Result, 0.001).
Result = 7.000014247557004.

?- squareroot(2, Result, 0.0001).
Result = 1.4142156862745099.




Handling Edge Cases

The number X must be greater than zero (X > 0).
The smaller the Accuracy, the more precise the result will be.
Large values of X may require more iterations to converge


